== address (15 cases) ==
  PASS absolute line number
  PASS . addresses the current line
  PASS $ addresses the last line
  PASS % addresses the whole buffer (1,$)
  PASS /re/ searches forward
  PASS ?re? searches backward
  PASS .+N relative address
  PASS .-N relative address
  PASS an explicit , range (2,4d)
  PASS bare , (both sides omitted) is `.,.` in ex, not `1,$`
  PASS a non-bare ; range separator
  PASS bare ; (both sides omitted) is `.,.` in ex, not `.,$`
  PASS 'x addresses a mark
  PASS default addressing: a bare command uses the current line
  PASS an out-of-range address is a silent refusal
== badcmd (15 cases) ==
  PASS cxyz is refused, not run as change
  PASS dxyz is refused, not run as delete
  PASS axyz is refused, not run as append
  PASS ixyz is refused, not run as insert
  PASS pxyz is refused, not run as print
  PASS wxyz is refused, not run as write
  PASS qxyz is refused, not run as quit
  PASS sxyz is refused, not run as substitute
  PASS jxyz is refused, not run as join
  PASS uxyz is refused, not run as undo
  PASS nux is refused (nu is the shortest-spelling of number)
  PASS = takes no operand
  PASS p refuses an operand maux has no handler for
  PASS z takes only a count
  PASS an unknown word with no verb letter is refused
== copy (1 cases) ==
  PASS t copies a line after the destination address
== delete (2 cases) ==
  PASS d deletes the addressed line
  PASS d over an explicit 2,4 range
== entry (3 cases) ==
  PASS a appends after the addressed line
  PASS i inserts before the addressed line
  PASS c changes (replaces) the addressed line
== file (12 cases) ==
  PASS n is RESERVED in ex, and refused SILENTLY in script mode
  PASS r reads a file in without rebinding the remembered name
  PASS e on a dirty buffer without ! is refused
  PASS e! forces a load, discarding a dirty buffer
  PASS w with no edits persists the buffer unchanged
  PASS w to an explicit, NEW different path leaves the loaded file alone
  PASS w refuses to overwrite an EXISTING different path without !
  PASS q quits with no unsaved changes
  PASS q! force-quits, discarding an unsaved edit
  PASS wq writes then quits
  PASS x writes (buffer dirty) then quits
  PASS x on a clean buffer still quits
== gap (2 cases) ==
  PASS j joins two lines WITH a separating space
  PASS .,$j joins current line through EOF, same space gap
== global (3 cases) ==
  PASS g/re/p prints every matching line
  PASS g/re/s//X/ -- widened g/v running a sub on every match
  PASS v/re/p prints every NON-matching line
== mark (1 cases) ==
  PASS kX sets a mark, 'x addresses it (incl. shift-on-delete)
== move (1 cases) ==
  PASS m moves a line after the destination address
== names (24 cases) ==
  PASS append / ap
  PASS insert / in
  PASS change / ch / cha
  PASS delete / de / dele
  PASS print / pr
  PASS list / li
  PASS number / nu
  PASS copy / co / t, with and without a blank
  PASS move / mo
  PASS join / jo
  PASS undo / un / und
  PASS substitute / su / sub
  PASS write / wr
  PASS xit / xi / x!
  PASS x! writes like x
  PASS wq! writes like wq
  PASS global / gl
  PASS vglobal / vg
  PASS mark / ma / k, a blank before the letter or none
  PASS mark with the full word
  PASS k with a blank before its letter
  PASS read / re
  PASS quit / qu on a clean buffer
  PASS a blank between the address and the command word
== print (6 cases) ==
  PASS p prints the addressed line
  PASS # numbered-print
  PASS nu is a numbered-print alias
  PASS l lists a line with a trailing $ (nonprinting marker)
  PASS z opens a window at the addressed line
  PASS = prints the addressed line's number
== substitute (6 cases) ==
  PASS s basic
  PASS s with /g substitutes every match
  PASS s with /p prints the result line
  PASS s/re/repl/N means "N LINES", not "the Nth match "in a line"
  PASS s//repl/ reuses the last search pattern
  PASS & repeats the last substitution
== undo (1 cases) ==
  PASS u undoes only the most recent change (single-level)

